 \begin{abstract}
-The reciprocal chiral exchange candidate for the Tri-Octagon model couples a native state to a periodic carrier and a reversible receiver through two finite distributed profiles. Its earlier analysis established initial supply from an empty carrier and receiver, and native credit from separately prepared states. We prove a sufficient finite-coupling theorem that connects these signs on one unchanged solution. Fix the admissible geometry, positive storage scales and native coupling, and a nonzero coherent launch. An explicit finite receiver-coupling threshold ensures initial positive total native power and receiver charging, followed by strictly negative total native-to-carrier power at a specified later time. The proof uses exact time and state scaling, a weighted external-energy estimate, and an oscillator comparison whose propagation and native-drift errors are bounded at finite coupling. Only first weak derivatives of the tent profiles are required. Continuity supplies a later interval of positive native-storage recovery without resetting the state or changing coefficients. Cumulative native debit remains nonnegative, and the coherent-core invariant ceiling persists. The result establishes a behavior of the published candidate, with a dynamically active receiver; it does not classify all positive couplings or establish a substantial recovered fraction. The witness describes fast collocated exchange rather than a completed spatial circuit or calibrated physical energy transport.
